To validate our intuitions, we compare width scaling under different network depths and resolutions, as shown in Figure 4. If we only scale network width wwithout changing depth ( d { = } 1 . 0 )and resolution ( r { = } 1 . 0 ), the accuracy saturates quickly. With deeper ( d { = } 2 . 0 )and higher resolution ( r { = } 2 . 0 ), width scaling achieves much better accuracy under the same FLOPS cost. These results lead us to the second observation:

Observation 2 – In order to pursue better accuracy and efficiency, it is critical to balance all dimensions of network width, depth, and resolution during ConvNet scaling.

In fact, a few prior work (Zoph et al., 2018; Real et al., 2019) have already tried to arbitrarily balance network width and depth, but they all require tedious manual tuning.

In this paper, we propose a new compound scaling method, which use a compound coefficient \phito uniformly scales network width, depth, and resolution in a principled way:

Equation: \text {d e p t h}: d = \alpha^ {\phi}

Equation: \text {w i d t h}: w = \beta^ {\phi}

Equation: \text {r e s o l u t i o n}: r = \gamma^ {\phi} \tag {3}

Equation: \mathrm {s . t .} \alpha \cdot \beta^ {2} \cdot \gamma^ {2} \approx 2

Equation: \alpha \geq 1, \beta \geq 1, \gamma \geq 1
